        <p>Camping in Sri Lanka is one of the most underrated ways to experience the island. While most visitors stay in guesthouses and hotels, a small number of self-sufficient travellers — and an increasing number of glampers — are discovering what it means to sleep under a canopy of stars above the Knuckles Range, wake to elephants drinking at a Udawalawe reservoir, or fall asleep to the waves on a remote beach near Nilaveli. Sri Lanka is not a country with a mature camping infrastructure. Facilities are sparse, permits are bureaucratic, and most sites require advance planning. But the rewards — landscapes, wildlife and silence that the guesthouse circuit rarely offers — are extraordinary.</p>

        <p>This guide covers every realistic camping option in Sri Lanka: from permitted wild camping in national park buffer zones to organised eco-camps and glamping lodges, plus the practicalities of permits, gear and safety around wildlife.</p>

