        <h2 id="knuckles">Knuckles Mountain Range</h2>
        <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <img src="/images/waterfall-hiking.webp" alt="Forest trail and waterfall in the Knuckles Mountain Range, Sri Lanka" class="w-full object-cover max-h-72" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">The Knuckles Conservation Forest offers multi-day trekking with overnight camping in mountain meadows</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>The Knuckles Conservation Forest — a UNESCO World Heritage buffer zone northeast of Kandy — is the finest wild camping destination in Sri Lanka. The range takes its name from its knuckle-like peaks, which rise to 1,863 metres above sea level, and the landscape shifts dramatically as you climb: from lowland rice paddies and forest villages through dense jungle and cloud forest to exposed grassland (patana) with views across multiple mountain ridges.</p>
        <p>Multi-day trekking routes allow overnight camping in mountain meadows. The most popular is the two-day route from Pitawala Pathana via Corbett's Gap to Mini World's End — a walk that covers jungle, open meadow and a viewpoint rivalling Horton Plains. Experienced trekkers also attempt the full Knuckles traverse, a three-to-four-day route requiring a guide and proper camping kit. Wildlife includes leopards (rarely seen), giant squirrels, purple-faced langur monkeys, endemic lizards and over 130 bird species including the Sri Lanka bush warbler and yellow-eared bulbul.</p>
        <p><strong>Permits:</strong> Camping is permitted with a Forest Department permit, available from the Divisional Forest Office in Kandy (approximately LKR 500–1,000 per person per night). A permit-registered guide is compulsory for most camping routes — trails are not reliably marked and weather can deteriorate rapidly. Arrange permits and guides through tour operators in Kandy or directly with the Forest Department; bookings two to three weeks in advance are advisable in high season (December–March).</p>
        <p><strong>Base towns:</strong> Kandy (1.5 hours by road) or Matale (45 minutes). Most guided Knuckles camping trips are arranged from Kandy.</p>

        <h2 id="adams-peak">Adam's Peak — Base Camp Overnight</h2>
        <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <img src="/images/adams-peak.webp" alt="Adam's Peak (Sri Pada) sacred mountain in the Hill Country, Sri Lanka" class="w-full object-cover max-h-72" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">The forest at the base of Adam's Peak offers a different camping experience to the pilgrimage summit climb</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Most visitors to <a href="/places/adams-peak">Adam's Peak</a> focus on the overnight summit climb — the legendary pilgrimage ascent that begins at 2am and reaches the sacred footprint at sunrise. But the forest around the base of Sri Pada offers a completely different kind of camping experience: peaceful, biodiverse jungle with the sound of waterfalls and the possibility of rare bird sightings, without the crowds of the pilgrimage trail.</p>
        <p>Several basic camp sites exist in the forest below the peak, particularly around Dalhousie and the Sri Pada Wildlife Sanctuary. This is best organised through a specialist eco-camping operator; independent wild camping in the sanctuary itself is restricted. The season for any camping here is December to May — outside the pilgrimage season, the forest is quieter and wildlife more active.</p>
        <p>For those who do want to combine the summit climb with a camping experience, some guides will arrange overnight stays at forest camps near the Dalhousie trailhead rather than the tea-house rest stops on the route — a much quieter and more atmospheric option.</p>

        <h2 id="horton-plains">Horton Plains — Camping Near Ohiya</h2>
        <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <img src="/images/horton-plains.webp" alt="Open highland plateau and World's End escarpment, Horton Plains National Park, Sri Lanka" class="w-full object-cover max-h-72" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">The grasslands around Horton Plains are among the coldest and most dramatic landscapes in Sri Lanka</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Camping inside <a href="/places/horton-plains">Horton Plains National Park</a> itself is not permitted for general visitors, but the area around Ohiya — the small railway station and village below the plateau — offers the closest camping experience to this extraordinary landscape. The highland grassland here sits above 2,000 metres and temperatures at night can drop to 5–8°C, making it one of the few places in Sri Lanka where you will genuinely be cold in your sleeping bag.</p>
        <p>A handful of basic guesthouses and small-scale camps around Ohiya allow you to wake early and reach the Horton Plains entrance at opening time (6am) before the day-trip crowds arrive from Nuwara Eliya and Ella — a significant advantage for seeing World's End before the clouds close in (typically by 9am). The Ohiya approach also gives access to Baker's Falls from a different angle and allows longer walks in the surrounding cloud forest.</p>
        <p>The Horton Plains and surrounding ridge are home to sambar deer, fishing cats, giant squirrels and an exceptional range of highland birds including the dull-blue flycatcher, Sri Lanka white-eye and yellow-eared bulbul. Pre-dawn is the best time for wildlife.</p>

