        <h2 id="yala">Yala National Park — Buffer Zone Eco-Camps</h2>
        <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <img src="/images/yala.webp" alt="Yala National Park jungle landscape and wildlife, Sri Lanka" class="w-full object-cover max-h-72" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">Yala's buffer zone eco-camps place you at the edge of one of the world's densest leopard habitats</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Camping inside <a href="/places/yala">Yala National Park</a> itself is not available to independent tourists, but the buffer zone around the park — particularly on its northern and western edges — hosts several eco-camps that offer a genuine wilderness sleep without requiring you to organise everything yourself. These are not primitive camps: most have furnished safari tents with proper beds, attached bathrooms and campfire cooking areas. What they offer is the sound of the bush at night — jackals, jungle fowl, the occasional elephant moving through the scrub — and the ability to enter the park at dawn without the long drive from Tissamaharama.</p>
        <p>The most atmospheric are camps positioned along dry river beds or at forest clearings, where elephant sightings from the camp itself are possible in the early morning and late evening. Look for operators who offer morning and evening guided walks around the buffer zone as part of the stay — these walks, outside the formal park boundary, are often as rewarding as a jeep safari inside.</p>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> First-time campers who want a comfortable introduction to sleeping in the bush; wildlife photography at golden hour.</p>

        <h2 id="wilpattu">Wilpattu National Park — Buffer Zone</h2>
        <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <img src="/images/wilpattu.webp" alt="Wilpattu National Park lake and jungle, Sri Lanka" class="w-full object-cover max-h-72" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">Wilpattu's famous villus — natural lake basins — attract leopards, sloth bears and elephants at dusk</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p><a href="/places/wilpattu">Wilpattu National Park</a> is Sri Lanka's largest national park and arguably its most atmospheric — a vast, ancient wilderness of scrub jungle, grassland and natural lake basins (called villus) that attract leopards, sloth bears, elephants and an extraordinary range of waterbirds. It reopened to tourists relatively recently after years of closure during the civil war and remains far less visited than Yala, making buffer zone camping here a genuinely off-the-beaten-track experience.</p>
        <p>A small number of eco-camps and tented lodge operations have opened in the Wilpattu buffer zone in recent years, particularly on the southern and eastern approaches to the park. These tend to be smaller and less polished than the Yala operations, but that is part of the appeal — you are likely to be the only guests, the nights are very quiet, and leopard sightings at the villus are a realistic possibility, particularly at dusk and dawn.</p>
        <p><strong>Getting there:</strong> Wilpattu is approximately 30km north of Puttalam, accessible by road from Colombo (3 hours) or Anuradhapura (1.5 hours). The remoteness is part of the experience.</p>

        <h2 id="udawalawe">Udawalawe Buffer Zone</h2>
        <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <img src="/images/udawalawe.webp" alt="Elephants at Udawalawe National Park reservoir, Sri Lanka" class="w-full object-cover max-h-72" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">Udawalawe is home to over 600 wild elephants — buffer zone camps offer close encounters at dusk</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Udawalawe National Park is Sri Lanka's best destination for elephant watching — over 600 wild elephants live here year-round, and sightings are virtually guaranteed on any jeep safari. The buffer zone on the park's northern and western edges offers camping experiences close to the reservoir, where elephant herds come to drink at dusk in what can be one of the most spectacular wildlife moments on the island.</p>
        <p>A few small camps and eco-lodges in the Udawalawe buffer zone offer overnight stays with early-morning safari departures. Because Udawalawe's elephant population is so large and the terrain relatively open, the boundary between camp and wildlife is pleasantly blurry — elephants are often visible from camp without entering the park at all.</p>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Families and those whose primary goal is elephant watching; Udawalawe is an easier and more family-friendly camping option than Yala or Wilpattu.</p>

        <h2 id="kumana">Kumana National Park</h2>
        <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <img src="/images/kumana.webp" alt="Kumana National Park wetland and bird colony, Sri Lanka" class="w-full object-cover max-h-72" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">Kumana's mangrove lagoon hosts one of Asia's largest colonial waterbird nesting colonies</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Overnight camping inside <a href="/places/kumana">Kumana National Park</a> is the most remote and demanding camping experience in Sri Lanka, and for the right traveller, the most rewarding. Kumana is Sri Lanka's eastern wilderness — a vast stretch of wetland, dry forest and mangrove lagoon on the coast south of Arugam Bay — and the Department of Wildlife Conservation permits overnight camping inside the park with advance permits. This is primitive camping: no facilities, no running water, no electricity.</p>
        <p>What Kumana offers is extraordinary. The mangrove lagoon at the park's heart hosts one of Asia's most significant colonial waterbird nesting sites, with painted storks, night herons, little cormorants, spoonbills and open-billed storks nesting in their thousands. Leopards, elephants, crocodiles and endemic birds (the Kumana area has exceptional records for Sri Lanka's endemic species) are all present. The sense of solitude — of being genuinely alone in a vast, ancient landscape with no other humans visible — is rare anywhere in the world, let alone on a small and densely populated island.</p>
        <p><strong>Permits:</strong> Apply to the Department of Wildlife Conservation (DWC) head office in Colombo well in advance — at least four to six weeks. A ranger escort is compulsory for overnight stays. Budget for significant permit and escort fees.</p>

        <h2 id="beach">Beach Camping in Sri Lanka</h2>
        <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <img src="/images/arugam-bay.webp" alt="Arugam Bay beach and surf, east coast Sri Lanka" class="w-full object-cover max-h-72" width="800" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2 text-center">Arugam Bay and the remote beaches of the east coast are the best base for beach camping in Sri Lanka</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Sri Lanka's coastline — particularly the east coast — has stretches of beach that are genuinely remote outside the peak surf and tourist season. Organised beach camping is limited and informal, but several areas offer realistic options for self-sufficient campers.</p>
        <p><strong>Arugam Bay (East Coast):</strong> The surfing capital of Sri Lanka has a cluster of beach camps and basic eco-retreats south of the main bay — particularly around Pottuvil Lagoon and the beaches leading towards Panama. These are simple operations: a cleared beach area, fire pit, basic toilet facilities. The Pottuvil Lagoon itself backs onto the beach and is one of the best bird-watching sites in Sri Lanka, with flamingos, painted storks and breeding waterbirds visible from camp.</p>
        <p><strong>Nilaveli (North East Coast):</strong> The long empty beach north of Trincomalee is one of the most beautiful in Sri Lanka — calm, clear water and white sand backed by casuarina trees. A small number of basic beach camps operate here, particularly around the access points to Pigeon Island. The season is May to September (the north-east monsoon makes this coast rough from October to April).</p>
        <p><strong>Tangalle (South Coast):</strong> The south coast around <a href="/places/tangalle">Tangalle</a> has several small beach camps on the quieter stretches east of the town. These tend to be slightly more organised than the east coast options, with beach-side tent pitches, campfire cooking and snorkelling accessible from shore. Best season: December to April.</p>
        <p><strong>Important note:</strong> Wild beach camping (pitching a tent anywhere on a public beach) is not officially regulated but should be done with caution — speak to local guesthouses and check current conditions before pitching on an unmanaged stretch of coast.</p>

