        <h2 id="glamping">Glamping vs Wild Camping</h2>
        <p>Sri Lanka's camping scene splits clearly into two categories, and understanding the difference before you plan your trip avoids disappointment.</p>
        <p><strong>Glamping (Luxury Tented Camps):</strong> These are permanent or semi-permanent tented lodges — typically safari-style canvas tents on raised wooden platforms, with proper beds, linen, attached bathrooms and often a restaurant or dining area. You are sleeping in a tent, but with hotel-level comfort. These operate at Yala buffer zone, Wilpattu and some highland areas. Expect to pay USD 80–250 per night. Advance booking is required. No camping equipment needed.</p>
        <p><strong>Eco-Camps (Basic but Organised):</strong> A step down from glamping — shared bathrooms, simpler tents or basic bungalows, camp-fire cooking. These are found around Knuckles, Arugam Bay and the east coast. Expect USD 20–60 per night. Your own sleeping bag may be useful.</p>
        <p><strong>Wild Camping (Self-Sufficient):</strong> Bringing your own tent, sleeping bag and all equipment, with advance permits, into designated camping areas within national park buffer zones or Forest Department land. This is the hardest to organise, requires the most preparation, and delivers the most genuine wilderness experience. Required for Knuckles multi-day treks and Kumana overnight stays.</p>

        <h2 id="best-time">Best Time to Camp in Sri Lanka</h2>
        <p>Sri Lanka's two monsoon seasons create clear camping windows — and the right time depends heavily on where you want to camp.</p>
        <p><strong>December to March (North-East Monsoon ends):</strong> The best all-round camping season. The south and west coasts are dry, the Hill Country is cool and clear, and Yala and Udawalawe are at peak wildlife density as water sources contract. Knuckles is accessible and at its most dramatic. Avoid the north and east coasts (still recovering from the north-east monsoon).</p>
        <p><strong>April to May (Inter-Monsoon):</strong> An excellent window for east coast camping before the south-west monsoon arrives. Arugam Bay comes alive in May. Temperatures are higher but manageable. Good for Kumana (elephant herds gather at shrinking water sources).</p>
        <p><strong>May to September (South-West Monsoon):</strong> The west coast, Hill Country and Yala become wetter and more difficult. The east coast is at its best — Arugam Bay, Nilaveli and Kumana are all excellent. Knuckles is more challenging but still doable with a good guide and rain-proof kit.</p>
        <p><strong>October to November (Inter-Monsoon):</strong> Transitional and unpredictable — both coasts can receive rain. Generally not the best time for camping unless you are very experienced and well-equipped.</p>
        <p><strong>Highland note:</strong> The Knuckles Range and Horton Plains area can be cold and wet at any time of year — even in the "dry" season, cloud and mist are common at elevation. Always bring waterproofs and a warm layer regardless of the season.</p>

        <h2 id="permits">How to Get Camping Permits</h2>
        <p>This is the most practically daunting aspect of camping in Sri Lanka, and it pays to start early.</p>
        <p><strong>National Park Camping (Kumana, Udawalawe buffer zone, Wilpattu buffer zone):</strong> Apply to the Department of Wildlife Conservation (DWC) head office in Battaramulla, Colombo. You will need to submit a written application with your passport details, proposed dates and group size. A local tour operator or guide can handle this process on your behalf, which is strongly recommended. Budget at least four to six weeks lead time, and have backup dates available. Fees vary by park and group size; ranger escort fees are additional.</p>
        <p><strong>Knuckles Forest Department Permits:</strong> Apply to the Divisional Forest Office in Kandy (located on Rajapihilla Mawatha). Permits cost approximately LKR 500–1,000 per person per night. A registered guide is compulsory. Most Kandy-based trekking operators can arrange permits as part of a package, which is the simplest approach.</p>
        <p><strong>Eco-Camps and Glamping:</strong> These are privately operated and do not require separate permits — book directly through the operator or via Booking.com or similar. The operator handles all required park and buffer-zone permissions on your behalf.</p>
        <p><strong>Beach Camping:</strong> No specific national permit is required for camping in non-restricted coastal areas, but check with the local police station or tourism authority if you are planning to camp on a stretch of beach that is not managed by an operator — regulations vary by district.</p>

        <h2 id="gear">Essential Gear for Camping in Sri Lanka</h2>
        <p>Sri Lanka's climate and terrain require some specific kit choices, particularly if you are planning highland or jungle camping rather than an organised eco-camp.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tent:</strong> A quality three-season tent rated for wind and rain. Sri Lankan weather can surprise even in the dry season — don't bring a festival tent.</li>
          <li><strong>Sleeping bag:</strong> For highland camping (Knuckles, Horton Plains area), bring a bag rated to at least 5°C. For lowland and beach camping, a lightweight 15°C bag or even a silk liner may be sufficient.</li>
          <li><strong>Sleeping mat:</strong> Ground insulation is important in highland areas. A self-inflating mat or foam roll is sufficient.</li>
          <li><strong>Water purification:</strong> Water tablets or a filter is essential for wild camping — don't rely on being able to buy bottled water in remote areas.</li>
          <li><strong>Head torch:</strong> Essential. Bring spare batteries.</li>
          <li><strong>Insect repellent:</strong> DEET-based repellent at 30%+ concentration. Leeches are present in jungle and highland areas (particularly in wet season); tuck trousers into socks and apply repellent to boots and socks as well as skin.</li>
          <li><strong>Waterproofs:</strong> A lightweight rain jacket and waterproof bag cover for your pack. Essential in the Knuckles.</li>
          <li><strong>First aid kit:</strong> Include antiseptic for leech bites and minor cuts, blister plasters, rehydration sachets and any personal medication.</li>
          <li><strong>Camp shoes or sandals:</strong> To change into at camp — wearing wet boots all evening is miserable.</li>
          <li><strong>Dry bags:</strong> For electronics and spare clothing in jungle and highland areas.</li>
          <li><strong>Wildlife whistle or air horn:</strong> For buffer zone camps near elephants — a deterrent to use if an elephant approaches camp (your guide will advise on protocol).</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="safety">Safety Around Wildlife</h2>
        <p>Sri Lanka's camping areas bring you into proximity with animals that can be genuinely dangerous. The following is not optional advice.</p>
        <p><strong>Elephants:</strong> Wild elephants kill more people in Sri Lanka each year than any other animal. Buffer zone camps near Yala, Wilpattu and Udawalawe are in areas where elephants move freely. Always follow your guide or camp host's instructions exactly. Never approach an elephant on foot. If an elephant enters the camp area at night, stay inside your tent (or in a solid structure if available), make no sudden movements and no noise, and wait for it to move on. Do not run — elephants can move at 25km/h and running triggers a chase response.</p>
        <p><strong>Leopards:</strong> Leopards are present at Yala, Wilpattu, Knuckles and the buffer zones of several other parks. Attacks on humans are extremely rare, but leopards are active at night and should not be approached. Keep food inside sealed containers and never leave food outside your tent overnight — it attracts scavengers, which can in turn attract larger predators.</p>
        <p><strong>Crocodiles:</strong> Mugger crocodiles are present in rivers, lakes and lagoons throughout the lowland parks and coastal areas. Do not swim in freshwater bodies at national parks or in remote coastal lagoons. Wash at least 10 metres back from any water source if camping near rivers or lakes.</p>
        <p><strong>Snakes:</strong> Sri Lanka has several highly venomous snake species including the Russell's viper, saw-scaled viper and king cobra. Always check inside shoes before putting them on in the morning. Watch where you step at night. If someone in your group is bitten, immobilise the limb, keep the patient calm and horizontal, and get to a hospital immediately — do not cut, suck or apply a tourniquet. Anti-venom is available at district hospitals.</p>
        <p><strong>Scorpions:</strong> Common in dry lowland areas. Shake out bedding and clothing before use. Painful but rarely life-threatening for adults.</p>

        <h2 id="tips">Practical Tips</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Apply for national park permits at least four to six weeks in advance — earlier for peak season (December–March)</li>
          <li>Hire a local guide for any highland or wilderness camping — trail marking is minimal and wildlife knowledge is invaluable</li>
          <li>Tell someone at your guesthouse or hotel your exact route and expected return time before any wild camping trip</li>
          <li>Mobile phone coverage is surprisingly good even in remote areas of Sri Lanka, but does not extend to all parts of the Knuckles Range or Kumana interior — download offline maps (Maps.me or OsmAnd work well)</li>
          <li>Carry enough food for an extra day on any multi-day trek — weather delays are common</li>
          <li>Practice Leave No Trace: carry all waste out with you; use biodegradable soap at least 50 metres from water sources; do not collect plants or disturb wildlife</li>
          <li>Camping fires are not permitted in national parks or Forest Department areas; portable camp stoves are acceptable</li>
          <li>Travel insurance that specifically covers camping, trekking and wildlife areas is strongly recommended</li>
          <li>The Knuckles Range requires Forest Department permits available from the Divisional Forest Office in Kandy — your guide can arrange these but you should confirm in advance</li>
          <li>For overnight stays near elephants, keep your camp tidy and never leave food outside — a curious elephant in a camp is extremely dangerous</li>
        </ul>

